[38;2;124;124;124mk?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about why this is impossible.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. The number of zeros with imaginary part between 0 and T is approximately N(T)[0m[38;2;124;124;124m= (T/(2π)) log(T/(2π)) - T/(2π) + O(log T).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m2. If zeros were at s = 1/2 + ik for positive integers k, then the number of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mzeros with imaginary part between 0 and T would be approximately T (the number [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mof positive integers up to T).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m3. But N(T) ~ (T/(2π)) log(T/(2π)), which grows like T log T, much faster than [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mT.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the density of zeros is wrong. The zeros are much denser than integers. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnumber of zeros up to height T grows like T log T / (2π), while the number of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpositive integers up to T is just T. So there are far more zeros than integers, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mmeaning zeros cannot all be at integer imaginary parts.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mMore precisely: N(T) = (T/(2π)) log(T/(2π)) - T/(2π) + O(log T). For large T, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthis is ~ (T log T)/(2π), which exceeds T for T > e^{2π} ≈ 535. So for T large [0m
[38;2;124;124;124menough, there are more zeros than positive integers up to T, contradiction.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be more careful. The zeros come in conjugate pairs, and there [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mare also zeros with negative imaginary part. The zeros at s = 1/2 + ik for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpositive integers k - these are on the critical line at integer heights.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe number of zeros with 0 < Im(s) < T (counting the upper half) is N(T) ~ (T/[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(2π)) log(T/(2π)) - T/(2π).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf all non-trivial zeros were at s = 1/2 + ik, k positive integer, then the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnumber with 0 < Im(s) ≤ T would be ⌊T⌋ (the number of positive integers ≤ T).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor large T, (T/(2π)) log(T/(2π)) - T/(2π) grows like T log T while ⌊T⌋ ~ T. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSince T log T / (2π) >> T for large T, we have N(T) >> ⌊T⌋, contradiction.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a clean argument. Let me also note that even the first few zeros are [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mknown not to be at integer heights: the first zero is at approximately 1/2 + [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m14.1347i, not at an integer. But the density argument is more fundamental and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdoesn't rely on computation.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me also think about whether we even need the Riemann-von Mangoldt [0m[38;2;124;124;124mformula. There's a simpler argument potentially.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually the density/counting argument is the cleanest. Let me write it up.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Riemann-von Mangoldt counting formula: N(T) = (T/(2π)) log(T/(2π)) - T/(2π) [0m[38;2;124;124;124m+ O(log T), where N(T) counts zeros with 0 < Im(s) ≤ T (in the critical strip, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwith multiplicity).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf all non-trivial zeros were at s = 1/2 + ik, k = 1, 2, 3, ..., then N(T) = ⌊T⌋[0m[38;2;124;124;124mfor all T ≥ 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut N(T) = (T/(2π)) log(T/(2π)) - T/(2π) + O(log T) → ∞ faster than T. [0m
